#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313808 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e367aafb-2474-5dfc-a45f-be3a5ea76942" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating the Solution to an Initial Value Problem Using Euler's Method With Two Steps"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e0a1b3ef-a147-5017-86db-ae13d3616125" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313810} {:answer/id #uuid "0bdd75aa-971c-554a-a4f5-bffc90dea1fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313811} {:answer/id #uuid "4468d990-b506-526f-b8d2-e3701388d6c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313812} {:answer/id #uuid "a27a4348-b482-542b-90d5-db70370d9e7c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{17}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313813} {:answer/id #uuid "c49fb292-6c15-5813-86e9-14c0376c444f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313814}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a27a4348-b482-542b-90d5-db70370d9e7c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{17}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313813} :answer-field/id #uuid "b9b36b11-1994-515a-90ae-54a5da40dd35" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313809}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5237bd2c-76dc-568c-82b6-a0490e966574" :question/math-academy-id "q-53864" :question/problem "Given the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=x+y,\\,y(3)=2,\n$$\n\nuse Euler's method with two steps of equal size to approximate $y(4).$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that we are given $y(0)$ and asked to find $y(1).$ The distance from $x=3$ to $x=4$ is $1,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be  \n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{4-3}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with Euler's method. We'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(3)=2.$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $3$ | $2$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n \n\n**Step 1.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the first row) and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x+y=3+2=5 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=5\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=3+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{7}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=2+\\frac{5}{2}=\\frac{9}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $3$ | $2$ | $5$ | $\\frac{5}{2}$ |\n| $\\frac{7}{2}$ | $\\frac{9}{2}$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 2.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row) and new values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x+y=\\frac{7}{2}\\cdot \\frac{9}{2}=8 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=8\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=4 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=\\frac{7}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=4 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=\\frac{9}{2}+4=\\frac{17}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $3$ | $2$ | $5$ | $\\frac{5}{2}$ |\n| $\\frac{7}{2}$ | $\\frac{9}{2}$ | $8$ | $4$ |\n| $4$ | $\\frac{17}{2}$ | $$ | $$ |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that  $y(4)≈\\frac{17}{2}.$"}]}
